# Variant binaries built with `make EXE=...` while another run holds the
# default one.  The pattern is deliberately broad: this session committed
# a 131MB executable twice, once as ccx_stepB and once as ccx_glob, and
# both times the remote's pre-receive hook caught what .gitignore and I
# did not.  Sources here are .c/.f/.h/.inc, so an extensionless ccx_* is
# always a binary.
ccx_step*
ccx_glob
# And the self test runner, which is the same 113MB of statically linked MKL
# as the solver.  That is THREE times now - ccx_stepB, ccx_glob, and this -
# and each time .gitignore was extended by one name afterwards, which is a
# rule that only ever covers the binary that already got through.  The
# generic form cannot be `ccx_*': that matches ccx_2.23.c and would ignore
# source.  What actually stops it is the Makefile's own clean target and
# `git status' before `git add -A'; the names below are the backstop.
ccx_selftest

# Generated by tools/mkswitches.py at session start.  The registry header
# src/ccxopt_list.h is tracked because it is compiled; the markdown beside
# it is a build artifact and this repository is kept free of markdown.
docs/SWITCHES.md
